Multiply numerator and denominator by \(\sqrt{12}\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\frac{6}{\sqrt{12}}\cdot\frac{\sqrt{12}}{\sqrt{12}} &= \frac{6\sqrt{12}}{12}\\
&= \frac{\sqrt{12}}{2}
\end{aligned}
\]
Since \(\sqrt{12}=2\sqrt{3}\),
\[
\frac{\sqrt{12}}{2}=\frac{2\sqrt{3}}{2}=\sqrt{3}
\]
Therefore, the simplified rationalized form is \(\sqrt{3}\). The alternative retains an extra numerical factor.

Multiply numerator and denominator by \(\sqrt{2}\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\frac{2}{3\sqrt{2}}\cdot\frac{\sqrt{2}}{\sqrt{2}} &= \frac{2\sqrt{2}}{3\cdot 2}\\
&= \frac{2\sqrt{2}}{6}\\
&= \frac{\sqrt{2}}{3}
\end{aligned}
\]
Thus, the simplified rationalized form is \(\frac{\sqrt{2}}{3}\). The alternative drops a factor of two from the new denominator.

Multiply numerator and denominator by \(\sqrt{12}\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2\sqrt{12}}\cdot\frac{\sqrt{12}}{\sqrt{12}} &= \frac{\sqrt{36}}{2\cdot 12}\\
&= \frac{6}{24}\\
&= \frac{1}{4}
\end{aligned}
\]
Therefore, the simplified rationalized form is \(\frac{1}{4}\). The alternative keeps a radical that cancels between numerator and denominator.
